    <h2>Mowing Maintains a Yard; Landscaping Fixes One</h2>

    <p>Plenty of yards need nothing more than a crew that shows up every week and keeps the grass tidy, and there's no shame in selling exactly that. The mistake is assuming that's all landscaping is — that the only difference between a $35 mow and a real landscape crew is markup. It isn't. Mowing maintains a yard that's already working. Landscaping fixes a yard that isn't: the corner that stays soggy, the shady strip where grass won't grow, the bed that erodes down the slope every spring. Those are different skills, and treating them as the same thing is how homeowners end up paying a mowing crew for years to trim around a problem nobody's addressing.</p>

    <h2>What a Real Yard Problem Actually Is</h2>

    <p>The recurring money problems in Shoals yards trace back to water and soil, not grass length. The regional clay drains slowly, so grading is everything — a yard that pitches toward the house instead of away collects water at the foundation and stays wet long after the rain stops. Turf choice matters too: a warm-season grass baking in full sun and a shade-struggling patch under a mature oak in an older Florence neighborhood are opposite problems, and seeding both the same way fails both. The reality landscaping companies underplay is that the healthy-looking yard is usually the one where someone solved the drainage first and the grass second.</p>

    <h2>What the Good Crews Do Differently</h2>

    <p>A crew worth its price reads the yard before it quotes. They look at where water goes in a storm, whether the soil is compacted clay or amended bed soil, how much sun each zone actually gets across the day, and whether the grade sends runoff toward or away from the structure. On a drainage job they regrade and install a French drain or dry creek to carry water to the street or a low corner, instead of dumping topsoil to hide a low spot that refills next week. The honest admission most won't volunteer: a lot of what gets sold as "landscaping" is cosmetic, and the work that actually protects a property — grading and drainage — is the unglamorous part crews skip when they're racing to the next mow.</p>

    <h2>Red Flags in a Landscaping Bid</h2>

    <p>A bid that answers a standing-water complaint with "we'll bring in topsoil and reseed" is treating a symptom. A crew that won't walk the yard before quoting, or can't say what turf type they'd use and why, is guessing. And a maintenance quote that's dramatically cheaper than everyone else's usually means a mow-only crew that will trim around your real problems until you finally call someone else to fix them — at which point you've paid twice.</p>

    <h2 id="pricing-summary">What Landscaping Runs in the Shoals</h2>

    <p>Real ranges, and what moves them:</p>
    <ul class="trades-list-block">
      <li><strong>Recurring mow and maintenance</strong> — about $35 to $65 per visit for a typical lot, more on larger properties.</li>
      <li><strong>Bed install or refresh</strong> — roughly $800 to $3,000 depending on size and plant selection.</li>
      <li><strong>Drainage correction (regrade + French drain)</strong> — about $2,500 to $6,000, driven by how far water must be carried.</li>
      <li><strong>Design-and-install projects</strong> — up from there, priced by scope.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>The bigger, newer lots in the Muscle Shoals subdivisions tend toward larger maintenance and irrigation budgets; the established properties in Florence's older neighborhoods lean toward bed work, mature-tree care, and drainage on yards that were graded decades ago.</p>

    <h2>Playing It Smart on a Shoals Property</h2>

    <p>The smart sequence is to fix water first, soil second, and plants last, because plants installed over a drainage problem just die more expensively. If a storm leaves standing water for more than a day, that's the job to price before any planting. And matching the plan to the property pays off — a low-water, low-mow plan on a big open Muscle Shoals lot, careful bed and canopy work on an established Florence street near the UNA area where the yards are part of the neighborhood's character.</p>

